<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
    SMS.Web — the browser host. Server-rendered MVC with Razor and cookie authentication.

      May reference:      Application, Infrastructure.
      Must not reference: Api. Not as a project reference, and not over HTTP.

    That last rule is the one worth stating plainly, because the obvious-looking
    alternative is wrong here:

    SMS.Web MUST NOT call SMS.Api over HTTP. They are two hosts over the same application
    layer, not a client and a server. Routing the website through its own API would add a
    network hop, JSON serialisation and a second authentication mechanism inside what is,
    in Model A, a single machine in a school. Both hosts call the handlers in
    SMS.Application directly, in-process (architecture section 11).

    (A company project you may have seen does the opposite — its admin site calls its API
    over HTTP. That API is partner-facing, with request signing and scopes, and lives
    across a network boundary. Different problem, different answer.)

    This project is thin on purpose: controllers validate input, call one handler, and
    render a view. If logic starts accumulating here, it belongs in Application — the API
    will need it too.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <UserSecretsId>4a4b251b-307d-4933-b84e-29e4e4f64e49</UserSecretsId>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SMS.Application\SMS.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SMS.Infrastructure\SMS.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.AspNetCore" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
